# FastAPI

Builds on `code-python`. Apply when the project uses FastAPI. Prefer the
project's existing DI, settings, and ORM patterns — do not introduce a second
web stack unless asked.

## App structure

- Split routes into `APIRouter` modules; include them on the app with clear
  prefixes and tags
- Lifespan/startup: acquire shared resources once (DB pools, clients); shut
  them down cleanly
- Settings from environment via the project's settings model — no hardcoded
  secrets

## Request/response models

- Use Pydantic models (or the project's schema layer) for request bodies,
  query params, and response models
- Keep path/query/body types explicit; prefer `response_model` (or equivalent)
  so OpenAPI matches reality
- Validate at the boundary; business logic receives already-parsed types
- Do not return ORM objects directly unless the project has a deliberate,
  safe pattern for that

## Dependencies and auth

- Use `Depends` for auth, DB sessions, and shared services
- Enforce authorization in dependencies or services — not only in docs
- Prefer one session-per-request dependency the project already uses; close or
  remove sessions on teardown

## Async and I/O

- `async def` endpoints must not call blocking I/O directly; use async
  drivers or `run_in_threadpool` / project helpers for blocking work
- Prefer async clients for outbound HTTP when the endpoint is async
- Cancellation: honor disconnects when the project has a pattern for it

## Errors and status codes

- Raise `HTTPException` (or project exceptions) with correct status codes
- Use exception handlers for consistent error payloads
- Do not leak stack traces or internal hosts to clients in production paths

## Tests

- Prefer `TestClient` / `AsyncClient` and project fixtures; ship tests with
  behavior changes when a runner exists
- Override `Depends` in tests rather than patching internals when practical
